{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Give me the persons and the goods, etc.</b> One needs to know for what purpose he says to him <b>and the goods take for yourself</b> — is it not already taken by him? And he should have said nothing other than the thing he asks of him, that he give it to him by way of gift. For by his saying ״take for yourself״ he indicates that he needs to consent that he may take it, and this contradicts his saying ״give me the persons״?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the matter may be explained by way of what they said, of blessed memory, in the chapter הגוזל ומאכיל (Bava Kamma 116a), and this is their language: The Sages taught: A caravan that was traveling and a troop of soldiers fell upon it and seized it, and one person stood and rescued it — he rescued for the middle, meaning for half. And if he said ״I am rescuing for myself״ — he rescued for himself. And Rav Ashi explains: when the members of the caravan are able to rescue by exertion — therefore if he rescued without specification, it is for the middle, meaning: each one identifies what is his and takes it, as Rashi explained. But if he said ״I am rescuing for myself״ and the members of the caravan heard and remained silent, they revealed their mind that they had despaired and did not commit themselves, and he rescued for himself — end of quote. Now the king of Sodom held that he himself had not despaired and was still standing ready to rescue and hire armies against him. And when Abraham went to rescue, even though he might say that he revealed his mind that he was rescuing for himself — it is of no avail, for it requires that the king of Sodom hear his words and consent. Absent that, he rescued for the middle and would take only what is his own — namely Lot and his property. And you will find that the king of Sodom held himself in high regard, for so they said in the Midrash, and this is their language (Genesis Rabbah 43): ״וַיֵּצֵא מֶלֶךְ סְדוֹם״ etc. — he began wagging his tail: You went down into the fiery furnace and survived, and I went down into the bitumen and survived — end of quote. See how he held himself to be a man of ability." "<b>And with this</b> the verse <b>give me the persons</b> etc. is explained: meaning: as far as my reasoning is concerned, you rescued for the middle, for I did not despair, nor did I make it known to you that you are rescuing for yourself while I remained silent. But according to your reasoning, that you give a tithe, it appears that you hold there is no reality to my being able to rescue even by exertion, and in that case you have no need to reveal your mind. Therefore, in order that we not enter into a dispute, let us agree together to a compromise: you will waive your right and assign to me the persons, and I will waive my right and assign to you the goods — take them for yourself. Understand this. And he was the lesser one in error, for he is devoid of any rescue, and Rav Ashi distinguished only regarding a troop that seized and went its way. But in the situation before us, they had already engaged in war and been defeated, and there is no room for ״they are unable to rescue״ here, for they had already been reduced to nothing and had surely despaired. And it was with a false claim that he came wagging his tail. And you will find that Rav Ashi did not require him to say explicitly ״I am rescuing for myself״ except specifically when they are able to rescue by exertion. But if they are unable to rescue, he need not say ״for myself״ etc., and so it is explained there. If so, even if he were to say afterward ״I could have rescued,״ his claim is no claim, and so ruled the Rambam (Hilkhot Gezelah va-Avedah, chapter 12) and the Rosh." "<b>And you will find</b> that the Sages alluded to this matter with the sweetness of their language in saying ״wagging his tail,״ meaning: after he sees that his head has been taken off, he still wags his tail. For when he was in the full force of his kingship, and four kings who were with him could not stand before the four kings — how much the less, after the stripping of his pomp, could he possibly rescue! From this you learn that Abraham rescued for himself and acquired everything. And therefore he gave a tithe from everything — for even though he waived his claim in favor of the king of Sodom, a person is entitled to do with his own as he wishes, but not with the tithe, which belongs to the Most High."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:27.454997428Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}